  "working": "Roasting ZnS with O2 at >850°C gives ZnO (white zinc oxide). Reaction of ZnO with HCl yields ZnCl2 (X). Treatment of ZnCl2 with excess NaOH precipitates Zn(OH)2 (Y), which on heating may form ZnO again. Thus <W> = ZnO, <X> = ZnCl2, <Y> = Zn(OH)2 (or ZnO). Zn in ZnO is Zn(II) (oxidation state +2), not +1 → (A) false. Zn2+ has d10 configuration (no d electrons) → (B) true (metal in W has d10). Geometry around Zn in Zn(OH)2 or ZnO is typically tetrahedral/distorted tetrahedral, not square planar → (C) false. Zn2+ is d10, not d9 → (D) false. Hence only option B is correct.",
