  "working": "Reaction of carbon with sulfur at high temperature typically forms CS (carbonyl sulfide) or CS2? However, the most stable C–S product formed from solid carbon and sulfur at high temperature is CS (carbonyl sulfide) or possibly CS2 under certain conditions. CS has a bent shape due to the lone pair on sulfur, so option (A) could be considered correct. Reaction of hot coke (solid carbon) with steam (H2O) at 1000°C typically undergoes water-gas shift and produces CO and H2; the primary C-containing product is CO (carbon monoxide). CO has a bond order of 3 (triple bond), not 2.5, so option (B) is incorrect. CO is polar due to the C≡O bond dipole, so option (C) is incorrect. CO has no unpaired electrons (it is isoelectronic with N2, which has no unpaired electrons), so option (D) is correct. Therefore the correct option(s) are (D).",
